The paper identifies a genuine threat vector and makes sound design choices (batch poisoning, alternating training), but the headline results are substantially inflated by fixing trigger dimensions to the best-performing set—a fact the paper's own data (Table 2) makes starkly clear. The comparison with Baffle, while clearly categorized, is presented with a misleading "105% improvement" framing across different threat models. These are not minor presentation issues; they affect the core claim that TrojanTO is effective under realistic conditions. Compared to the 5.0-scoring DRL backdoor paper (NALkteEo9Q), which had similar result reliability concerns, and the 4.67-scoring cherry-picking paper (8FxELTdwJR), this paper has genuine contributions (the factor analysis and batch poisoning insight) but similarly inflated headline numbers. I place it slightly above the purely cherry-picking paper because the contribution is real and the methodology has sound components, but below the accepted papers because the experimental evidence doesn't support the generality of the claims.

